---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead title="Biography — Émile Savoie" description="Biography of Émile Savoie, teacher, composer, pianist, and flutist." />
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main>
			<div class="page-header">
				<div class="container">
					<h1>Biography</h1>
				</div>
			</div>

			<section class="section" style="padding-top: 2rem;">
				<div class="container">
					<div class="about">
						<div class="about__image">
							<img src="/images/emilesavoie.jpg" alt="Émile Savoie at the piano" loading="lazy" />
						</div>
						<div class="about__text">
							<p>
								Émile Savoie is a teacher, composer, pianist, and flutist born in
								Quebec in 1998. He studied in Montreal, where he earned his first
								piano diploma at the Cégep de Saint-Laurent and also studied
								harpsichord with Luc Beauséjour.
							</p>
							<p>
								He then pursued his studies at the University of Sherbrooke. There,
								he refined his piano playing under the guidance of Carmen Picard.
								He also performed as a percussionist in a symphony orchestra,
								taking part in major works such as Dvořák's <em>New World
								Symphony</em> and Tchaikovsky's <em>1812 Overture</em>. He
								completed a Certificate in Musical Studies (2019) followed by a
								Bachelor's degree in composition and music to image (2022) at the
								same institution. During his studies, he also served as a teaching
								assistant and tutor in analysis, orchestration, counterpoint, and
								electroacoustics.
							</p>
							<p>
								In 2024, he moved to Ontario to pursue a Master's degree in
								creative and collaborative performance at Wilfrid Laurier
								University, where he also worked as a graduate teaching assistant.
							</p>
							<p>
								His music balances sensitivity and methodism. Though firmly rooted
								in classical music, he naturally draws from a wide range of
								genres, allowing his work to evolve into a contemporary
								aesthetic. A portion of his compositions has been published by the
								Northern Lights Canadian National Conservatory of Music. Others
								have been performed by ensembles across Canada, including the
								Orchestre symphonique de Sherbrooke, the Laurier Wind Orchestra,
								and the Orchestre à Vent de Sherbrooke. Notable projects include
								the soundtrack for the short film <em>She Wants To Cry</em>
								(Festival of Recorded Movement, Vancouver, 2025), the video game
								<em>Ikolia</em> (Montreal, 2022), and the piece <em>Encre
								d'écorce</em>, broadcast on radio for the cultural event
								<em>Sherbrooke prend la parole</em> (2022) and performed live for
								the multidisciplinary concert <em>Cycles</em> (Waterloo, 2025).
							</p>
							<p>
								A passionate reader with a love for novels, theater, and poetry,
								Émile's artistic sensibility is deeply shaped by literature. This
								connection to language and storytelling naturally led him to
								teaching — a vocation he has carried from a young age. Today, he
								gives piano lessons to students of all ages.
							</p>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>
			</section>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>
